 "rule": "# The court count's rule — row MC, BA-6 (written before its data, November 17, 2026, ~23:05)\n\nThe find that prompted it: ten AI-played French campaigns of forty turns (the AI-France harness's ten seeds,\nambient base 0, every BA-6 seam up as the session's final tree) recorded **no French silence at all** — 181\nshared victories, 92 rescues, 55 brothers, 22 resentments of a junior, 30 escalations, 48 titles offered —\nwhile the AI courts recorded 20. The rule (`precedence._silent_comrades` + `if not won: _silence`) remembers a\nHostile comrade's silence only when the lead's battle was lost or hung; its namesake, Auerstedt, was a VICTORY\nwon outnumbered about two to one (Davout's 26,000 against Brunswick's ~60,000; Bernadotte's I Corps stood\nwithin reach and marched to neither field; Davout never forgave him). The candidate: the silence is ALSO\nremembered when the lead was hard pressed (his side's men fewer than the foe's — the rescue's own reading),\nwon or not. Seam `precedence.a_silence_is_remembered_when_pressed`, row SILENCE, last on the ladder.\n\nArms: **A** = SILENCE down (the silence as built), **B** = SILENCE up. Same ten seeds, same harness, same tree.\n\n1. **The Auerstedt rule lives:** arm B records at least one French silence over the ten seeds.\n2. **It does not swamp the court:** arm B's French silences are at most a quarter of arm B's French rescues\n   (a quarrel deepened by absence stays rarer than a quarrel mended by a corps that came).\n3. **The court stays alive** (read on both arms, reported): at turn 40 the French court's one-way views among\n   its marshals (the Emperor and captives excluded) hold at least one Devoted (+2) AND at least two Rival or\n   worse (≤ −1), on at least eight of the ten seeds.\n4. **The game holds:** with SILENCE up, the commanded band reads inside 23–28 on all three arms and the deploy\n   gate is MET (the session's measure chain, arm ALL, re-taken).\n\n**The pick:** arm B is built (the seam stays up) if clauses 1, 2 and 4 pass; otherwise it is closed (down in\nthe game, `BA6_CLOSED`, the measured arm only). Clause 3 is reported on both arms; if it fails on BOTH, the\ncourt's warmth is a defect of its own: its cause is read from the tallies and a cure is measured by its own\nrule written before that data, in this session, and the record says which.\n"
